12
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:50,710
First, let's take a look at the test time for IELTS reading.

13
00:00:51,470 --> 00:00:54,150
For paper-based tests, you have 60 minutes

14
00:00:54,150 --> 00:00:57,270
to read, answer questions and transfer your answers

15
00:00:57,270 --> 00:00:58,410
to the answer sheet.

16
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:01,040
Please note that in IELTS reading tests, 

17
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:03,320
we don't have 10 minutes to transfer answers to

18
00:01:03,650 --> 00:01:05,730
the answer sheet like in the listening tests.

19
00:01:06,290 --> 00:01:08,590
Therefore, you need to read and transfer your

20
00:01:08,590 --> 00:01:10,330
answers at the same time.

21
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:13,930
Similarly, for computer-based tests, you have 60

22
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:16,410
minutes to read and answer questions and you

23
00:01:16,410 --> 00:01:19,290
need to type your answers directly onto the computer.

24
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:22,010
Number of questions.

25
00:01:22,610 --> 00:01:25,070
About the number of questions, an IELTS reading

26
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:28,490
test has 40 questions, which are divided into

27
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:30,830
three passages with increasing difficulty.

28
00:01:31,870 --> 00:01:32,390
Purpose.

29
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:35,990
An IELTS academic reading test assesses the following

30
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:37,950
aspects of your reading skills.

31
00:01:38,630 --> 00:01:43,650
Identify the main idea, understand details, logical arguments

32
00:01:43,650 --> 00:01:49,670
and recognize the writer's opinion, attitude and purpose.

33
00:01:50,950 --> 00:01:52,770
Content of reading passages.

34
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:57,130
Next, the content of reading passages are often

35
00:01:57,130 --> 00:02:01,310
taken from books, journals, magazines and newspapers and

36
00:02:01,310 --> 00:02:04,750
written by native speakers in an academic style.

37
00:02:06,270 --> 00:02:09,810
These passages have been selected for non-specialist

38
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:13,530
audience, but are appropriate for people entering university

39
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:16,570
courses or seeking professional registration.

40
00:02:17,410 --> 00:02:19,150
Common question types.

41
00:02:19,770 --> 00:02:22,410
About common question types in IELTS reading tests,

42
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:24,750
you have to understand all of them.

43
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:27,250
Number one, completion.

44
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:32,850
Sentence completion, table completion, flowchart completion, diagram

45
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:36,850
completion, summary completion and note completion.

46
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:41,230
Two, short answer questions.

47
00:02:42,210 --> 00:02:45,090
Answer questions with short words taken from the passage.

48
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:48,450
Three, matching.

49
00:02:49,290 --> 00:02:52,400
Matching headings, matching information

50
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,590
Matching features, matching sentence endings.

51
00:02:56,690 --> 00:02:59,310
Four, multiple choice questions.

52
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:04,270
Questions with single answer and questions with multiple answers.

53
00:03:05,390 --> 00:03:12,570
Five, True, False, Not Given and Yes, No, Not Given.

54
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:16,190
These question types will be explained in detail

55
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:19,370
later in the course and based on the order above.

56
00:03:20,010 --> 00:03:20,490
Stay tuned.

57
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:23,890
How to calculate IELTS reading band scores.

58
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:28,790
So, how can you calculate your IELTS reading band score?

59
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:31,370
Please look at the table of the reading

60
00:03:31,370 --> 00:03:33,990
band score in the IELTS academic test from

61
00:03:33,990 --> 00:03:36,210
the official website of IELTS.

62
00:03:37,170 --> 00:03:39,970
You already know how many correct answers you

63
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:41,550
have to get, don't you?

64
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:46,500
After having an overview of IELTS reading tests,

65
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:49,880
let's see what our reading course will provide you with.

66
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:51,280
Course goals.

67
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:55,600
Help you understand the format of IELTS reading tests.

68
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:59,160
Help you aim for a six or higher

69
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:01,300
reading band score after the course.

70
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,920
Help you master six necessary skills to improve

71
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:05,780
your reading skills.

72
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:09,680
Help you learn more than 100 B1 words

73
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:13,160
through reading passages suitable for intermediate learners.

74
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:21,280
Now, let's find out what the effective study

75
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:24,040
methods for this intermediate reading course are.

76
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:28,900
For today, I suggest you follow the following three steps.

77
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:30,680
Step one.

78
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:32,940
Watch the video attentively.

79
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:36,560
Take notes and complete tasks in the video.

80
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:38,680
Step two.

81
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:41,760
Do the exercises of each lesson.

82
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:45,540
And last but not least, step three.

83
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:50,040
Create a table of mistakes and a vocabulary list.

84
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:54,160
This third step is really important for both

85
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:57,240
your regular studies and exam preparation.

86
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:02,320
In short, steps one and two focus mostly

87
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:05,920
on task awareness, which is about getting used

88
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:09,640
to the test format, managing your time, and

89
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:11,000
checking your accuracy.

90
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:15,440
So, if you only focus on these tasks

91
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:18,040
and then move on to another reading passage,

92
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:21,520
you will not boost your vocabulary, which is

93
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:23,780
the key factor for score improvement.

94
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:27,820
That's why, for step three, we will delve

95
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:32,280
deeper into not only vocabulary expansion, but also

96
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:36,760
error analysis, where we synthesize the mistakes, helping

97
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:39,380
you pay more attention to them, to avoid

98
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:42,280
repeating the same mistakes in other exercises.

99
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:45,820
You can create a table of mistakes with

100
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:46,860
the following template.

101
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:51,180
In the column Date, you can write the

102
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:52,780
date when you did the test to keep

103
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:54,300
track of your learning progress.

104
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,720
In the column Passage, you can write the

105
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:00,720
lesson's name in this course, or the book title,

106
00:06:00,980 --> 00:06:03,680
unit title, the topic you are studying

107
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:06,200
in supplementary books to help you study the

108
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,780
course better, if you have any.

109
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:12,620
In the column Score, you can write your

110
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:15,400
score to self-evaluate your learning process.

111
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:19,400
In the column Question, you can write the

112
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:21,940
question number or a brief summary of the

113
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:25,140
question type so that you can easily review it later.

114
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:28,840
The column Mistake will be about the mistakes

115
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:32,340
you find after receiving corrections or self-checking the answers.

116
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:36,320
And the final column aims to suggest solutions.

117
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:39,620
By doing this, we can track our learning

118
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:42,480
progress over time to see if there are

119
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:47,020
any repeated mistakes, any score improvements, or new solutions.

120
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,940
Regarding making a vocabulary list, because in a

121
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:53,080
reading passage, there may be a lot of

122
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:55,400
new words, and it is very difficult for

123
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:57,620
us to memorize them all, or there are

124
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:00,960
specialized words which are a little practical, we

125
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:04,460
should prioritize learning vocabulary based on the following order.

126
00:07:05,980 --> 00:07:09,360
Learn words in questions and in information areas

127
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:11,260
containing the answers in paragraphs.

128
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:15,000
In the IELTS exam, the information in the

129
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,800
passage is often expressed in a different way

130
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:18,640
from the questions.

131
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:22,480
The act of using various expressions is called

132
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:23,660
paraphrase.

133
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:28,120
Therefore, you are learning and familiarizing yourself with

134
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:30,800
techniques for paraphrasing used by test makers.

135
00:07:31,420 --> 00:07:34,060
Also, you are learning how to apply them

136
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:35,640
to your writing skills.

137
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:40,300
For example, in the reading passage, there is

138
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:41,280
a question below.

139
00:07:42,540 --> 00:07:46,140
Any trees that are ... should be left to

140
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:49,400
grow as they add to the variety of

141
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:50,600
species in the forest.

142
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:56,300
The information area containing the answer in the paragraph is.

143
00:07:57,420 --> 00:08:00,540
Finally, rare species of trees in a forest

144
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:03,240
should also stay behind as they add to

145
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:05,200
its structural diversity.

146
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:12,460
The keyword variety in the question is similar to diversity.

147
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:15,600
If you don't know these two words, then

148
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:17,800
you should prioritize learning them.

149
00:08:18,640 --> 00:08:21,260
If you already know all those words, you

150
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:24,160
can spend time reading the whole passage and

151
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:26,460
learn essential words in sentences.

152
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,760
If you don't know the meaning of these

153
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:31,140
ones, it's really hard for you to guess

154
00:08:31,140 --> 00:08:34,260
or understand the meanings of the sentences containing them.

155
00:08:35,380 --> 00:08:38,140
Here is an example of how I organize

156
00:08:38,140 --> 00:08:39,659
my vocabulary list.

157
00:08:40,860 --> 00:08:43,800
In the column Word, you can write down

158
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:46,180
the word you need to learn, its word

159
00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:49,200
form and pronunciation to understand and use it

160
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:51,580
correctly in reading and other skills.

161
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:56,220
In the column Synonyms or Paraphrases in the

162
00:08:56,220 --> 00:08:58,840
paragraph, you can write down the word or

163
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:01,900
phrase the writer used to paraphrase the word

164
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:03,000
you need to learn.

165
00:09:04,020 --> 00:09:06,620
In the column Meaning, you can write down

166
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:10,760
its meaning in English and/or your first language.

167
00:09:11,540 --> 00:09:13,580
It would be best if you write it

168
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:15,940
in English because by doing that, you can

169
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:18,640
understand its meaning in the most correct way.

170
00:09:19,480 --> 00:09:22,860
In the column Example, you can form sentences

171
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:25,080
which are related to the topic you are

172
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:28,360
learning or use them in your writing and speaking.

173
00:09:28,980 --> 00:09:32,660
For example, in the vocabulary list, I wrote

174
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:35,380
a sample sentence for introduction of an IELTS

175
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:37,080
Writing task 2 essay.

176
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:40,240
It's like killing two birds in one stone,

177
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:41,080
isn't it?

178
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:44,680
You can also search for keywords of the

179
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:47,860
reading passages topic on Google or any task

180
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:50,980
2 question related to the topic to form

181
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:53,640
sentences with the words you've just learned and

182
00:09:53,640 --> 00:09:54,960
answer that question.

183
00:09:56,060 --> 00:09:58,560
These are the basic steps that help you

184
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:01,900
to make the most of the resources from

185
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:03,936
the IELTS intermediate reading course 

186
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:06,520
